---
import { BRAND, SCORE_SHORT } from '../lib/brand';

/**
 * WHO IS BEHIND THIS.
 *
 * The one thing Dan named as MISSING from the splash, at 02:02: *"I think the thing that's missing
 * is the credibility of who's behind it. So I would like to add another section of who's behind
 * the survey, and have our logos of Baseout, BuiltOnAir and On2Air."* He weighed modals for each
 * and then settled: *"maybe it's just enough for just the logo with some text and then a link that
 * takes you to them."* Then, on the compact sponsor line already at the foot of the page:
 * *"instead of this, that's not enough there."*
 *
 * So this is a section, not a line, and it takes the splash's footer slot: `index.astro` renders it
 * with `footer={false}`, per 09:05 (*"this footer can stay there like this for the form, just not
 * on the splash page"*). Every other page keeps the compact one.
 *
 * ── THE LOGOS, AND WHERE EACH ONE CAME FROM ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * All three are real marks now. The first pass set BuiltOnAir and On2Air in the brand's own type
 * because no asset existed in this repo; Oleh asked for the real ones and they were pulled from the
 * companies' own sites on 2026-08-27:
 *
 *   · Baseout    `apps/web/public/images/logo`, already two files, one per theme.
 *   · BuiltOnAir `builtonair.com/uploads/boa-web.png`, the header lockup. Cropped above the rule,
 *                dropping the "All things Airtable" tagline: at the size this row renders, a
 *                two-deck lockup makes the wordmark half the height of the other two.
 *   · On2Air     `on2air.com/logo-horizontal.png`. 212px wide is everything they publish, which is
 *                why the row renders short: at 1.55rem tall it is still better than 2x.
 *
 * THE DARK-THEME FILES ARE OURS, NOT THEIRS. Neither company publishes a reversed version, and
 * both marks are dark ink that disappears on this page's dark theme. The `-dark.png` files knock
 * the low-saturation ink to white and leave every saturated pixel untouched, so BuiltOnAir keeps
 * its bird and On2Air keeps its cyan "Air". It is the standard reversed treatment and it invents
 * nothing, but it is still a derived asset: if either brand kit has an official reversed lockup,
 * dropping it in over the file is the whole change.
 *
 * ── SIZING, AND IT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN EYEBALLED ──────────────────────────────────────────
 * The first pass picked three heights by eye and got them wrong in the same direction Oleh then
 * reported: Baseout smallest, BuiltOnAir largest. The cause is not padding. All three files are
 * already trimmed hard against their ink, so cropping them again changes nothing. It is that the
 * three lockups give the WORDMARK a different share of their own height:
 *
 *     Baseout      wordmark is 50% of the image (200 of 400) — its mark is twice the type
 *     On2Air       wordmark is 62% of the image (33 of 53)
 *     BuiltOnAir   wordmark is 74% of the image (102 of 137)
 *
 * So at one shared height BuiltOnAir's name renders half again bigger than Baseout's. `mark` sets
 * each image so the WORDMARK ink lands at the same height, 0.95rem, which is the thing the eye
 * compares when it decides two logos are "the same size". The numbers are 0.95 divided by the
 * share above; change the target and recompute, do not nudge them one at a time.
 *
 * ── THE SOURCE PARAMETER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * 02:56: *"that's where Baseout would, we could take them to a landing page that you'll be working
 * on. And that landing page, maybe we'll just have a variable that says like coming from the
 * survey, so we can maybe tailor it."* `?from=doap` is that variable. It only rides the Baseout
 * link: the other two are other people's sites and tagging their traffic is not ours to do.
 */

/** The landing page does not exist yet, so this points at the site and carries the tag regardless. */
const BASEOUT_HREF = 'https://baseout.com/?from=doap';

const PARTNERS = [
  {
    name: 'Baseout',
    href: BASEOUT_HREF,
    logo: '/logos/baseout-light.svg',
    logoDark: '/logos/baseout-dark.svg',
    width: 900,
    height: 240,
    mark: '1.9rem',
    role: 'Runs the survey',
    line: 'The backup and data intelligence platform for Airtable, launching this year.',
  },
  {
    name: 'BuiltOnAir',
    href: 'https://builtonair.com',
    logo: '/logos/builtonair-light.png',
    logoDark: '/logos/builtonair-dark.png',
    width: 928,
    height: 137,
    mark: '1.28rem',
    role: 'Runs the survey',
    line: 'The Airtable community’s podcast and network, week in and week out since 2019.',
  },
  {
    name: 'On2Air',
    href: 'https://on2air.com',
    logo: '/logos/on2air-light.png',
    logoDark: '/logos/on2air-dark.png',
    width: 212,
    height: 53,
    mark: '1.53rem',
    role: 'The team behind it',
    line: 'Has protected Airtable data for teams for years. The credibility anchor, not the sponsor.',
  },
];
---

<section class="cred" aria-labelledby="cred-h">
  <div class="cred__head">
    <div>
      <p class="bo-eyebrow">Who is behind it</p>
    {
      /* THE ARTICLE IS TYPED, THE NAME IS NOT. `BRAND` gained the word "Survey" on 2026-08-28 —
         Dan: *"it should never be 'State of DevOps for App Platforms', it should be survey,
         because the entity itself is the survey"* — and a name ending in a common noun wants a
         determiner in running prose in a way the old one could get away with without. */
    }
      <h2 id="cred-h" class="cred__h">The {BRAND} is run by people who have done this before</h2>
      <p class="cred__lede bo-measure">
        Nobody has benchmarked how teams protect the databases their business runs on. Three groups
        who have spent years inside this one are doing it together.
      </p>
    </div>

    {
      /*
        THE ASK, REPEATED, IN SPACE THIS BLOCK WAS ALREADY WASTING. The hero's button is above the
        fold and the next one is on `/thanks`; between them the page spends a screen on the
        explainer and another on the four things you get, so a reader convinced by those had to
        scroll back up to act. It stood alone under the rail for about ten minutes and read as a
        button abandoned in a field — Oleh, 2026-08-28: put it beside "Who is behind it" and save
        the space. The heading and its lede are held to `34rem` and `bo-measure`, so the right half
        of this row was empty at every desktop width; the pair fits there and costs no height at
        all.

        IT WAS BOTH BUTTONS UNTIL 2026-08-31, on the reasoning that a respondent who left mid-survey
        is exactly the person still reading this far down. That was wrong about the mechanism: on
        this device `/survey` resumes them without being asked, and on another device the start gate
        offers the link. The pair was solving a problem that had no case left.

        Verbatim from the hero: same label, same destination, same classes. A repeat ask that
        rewords itself reads as a second, different offer and makes the reader work out whether it
        is one.
      */
    }
    {/* THE SAME PAIR, THE SAME CUT. This block repeats the hero's ask where the reading ends, so it
        repeats the hero's shape exactly: one button, and no second door. See the long note in
        `HomeOpening.astro` for why `Resume your survey` went and why nothing replaced it. Two asks
        on one page that resolved their doors differently would be the worse version of the problem
        Dan was pointing at. */}
    <div class="cred__cta">
      <a class="bo-btn bo-btn--primary" href="/survey">Get your {SCORE_SHORT}</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <ul class="cred__list">
    {PARTNERS.map((p) => (
      <li class="cred__item">
        {/* Two files per logo and one of them is always display:none. The alternative is one file
            and a CSS filter, which would repaint BuiltOnAir's bird and On2Air's cyan along with the
            ink. The hidden one is `aria-hidden` and unnamed so the mark is announced once. */}
        <span class="cred__mark" style={`--mark-h: ${p.mark}`}>
          <img class="cred__img cred__img--light" src={p.logo} alt={p.name} width={p.width} height={p.height} loading="lazy" />
          <img class="cred__img cred__img--dark" src={p.logoDark} alt="" aria-hidden="true" width={p.width} height={p.height} loading="lazy" />
        </span>
        <p class="cred__role">{p.role}</p>
        <p class="cred__line">{p.line}</p>
        <a class="cred__link" href={p.href} rel="noopener">
          {p.name}
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <p class="cred__note">Answers are confidential and only appear in aggregate.</p>
</section>

<style>
  /* NO RULE ABOVE THIS SECTION. It had one, and Oleh took it off on 2026-08-27: the benefits rail
     directly above already closes itself with a full-width bottom border, so a second hairline a
     rhythm below it drew a band of nothing between two things that are simply consecutive. Air
     separates them now, which is what the rest of this page uses. */
  .cred {
    /* Was `var(--bo-space-7, 4rem)` — and `--bo-space-7` HAS NEVER EXISTED in this stylesheet, so
       this silently took the 4rem fallback and sat a rung off every other seam on the page. Now it
       is the same section seam as the rest. */
    margin-top: var(--bo-splash-slide);
  }

  /* THE PROSE TAKES THE MEASURE IT NEEDS AND THE BUTTONS TAKE WHAT IS LEFT. `1fr auto` rather than
     two equal halves: the heading is already capped at 34rem and the lede at `bo-measure`, so an
     equal split would only move the empty space around.

     `center`, not `end`. Sitting the pair on the lede's LAST line put it at the bottom of a
     three-part block and read as a footnote to the paragraph rather than as the section's ask —
     Oleh, 2026-08-28, who offered centre or the block's top edge. Top would have aligned two 44px
     buttons with the 11px eyebrow, the quietest thing in the column, and left them hanging above
     the heading. Centred, they land opposite the heading, which is the loudest, and the row reads
     as two halves of one thing. */
  .cred__head {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--bo-space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--bo-space-5);
  }

  /* ONE CHILD SINCE 2026-08-31, and this rule is nearly vestigial: `.cred__head` is a grid whose
     second track is `auto`, and `.bo-btn` is itself `display: flex`, so the button already sizes to
     its own content and sits centred in the row. An `align-items` here was measured at zero effect
     for every value it could take — the container is shrink-wrapped to the one thing in it — so it
     is gone rather than left reading as if it did something.

     What survives is `display: flex`, and only because the stacked override below needs a flex
     container to hand the button the full width. */
  .cred__cta {
    display: flex;
  }

  .cred__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--bo-space-2);
    max-width: 34rem;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-24);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .cred__lede {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  /* Three columns of equal weight, divided by rules rather than boxed. The same argument the
     benefits rail makes on this page: a rule groups as well as a card and promises less. */
  .cred__list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  .cred__item {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: var(--bo-space-2);
    padding: var(--bo-space-4) var(--bo-space-4) var(--bo-space-4) 0;
  }

  .cred__item + .cred__item {
    padding-left: var(--bo-space-4);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  /* A SHARED BOX WITH A PER-LOGO HEIGHT INSIDE IT. The box is one height for all three so the
     three roles, lines and links below start on the same baseline whatever the marks do; `--mark-h`
     then sets each logo inside it, because equal image heights do not mean equal apparent size. */
  .cred__mark {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: 2.25rem;
  }

  .cred__img {
    height: var(--mark-h, 1.7rem);
    width: auto;
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  .cred__role {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .cred__line {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  .cred__link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.375rem;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .cred__link:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .cred__note {
    margin: var(--bo-space-4) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-12);
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* THE THEME SWAP IS EXPLICIT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. The stored choice stamps `data-theme` on the
     root and the default setting stamps nothing, so the media query has to answer for "system" and
     the attribute has to be able to overrule it. Defining either one alone leaves one of the four
     combinations showing the wrong mark. */
  .cred__img--dark { display: none; }

  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root:not([data-theme='light']) .cred__img--light { display: none; }
    :root:not([data-theme='light']) .cred__img--dark { display: block; }
  }

  :root[data-theme='dark'] .cred__img--light { display: none; }
  :root[data-theme='dark'] .cred__img--dark { display: block; }
  :root[data-theme='light'] .cred__img--light { display: block; }
  :root[data-theme='light'] .cred__img--dark { display: none; }

  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    /* The pair drops under the prose rather than squeezing beside it: the heading is two lines of
       24px here and the two buttons are 300px of their own, which is more than the row has left.
       `justify-items: start` keeps them on the page's one left margin instead of stretching them
       across a width they did not ask for. */
    .cred__head {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      align-items: start;
      justify-items: start;
      gap: var(--bo-space-3);
    }

    .cred__list {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .cred__item {
      padding: var(--bo-space-4) 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    }

    .cred__item + .cred__item {
      padding-left: 0;
      border-left: 0;
    }

    .cred__item:last-child {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
  }

  /* Matches `.splash-cta` in `HomeOpening.astro`: below 30rem two buttons side by side each end up
     narrower than their own labels, so they stack and take the column. The two asks on this page
     must behave the same way or the repeat reads as a different control. */
  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    /* Stacked, the ask gets the full width it was denied at desktop: here it is the only thing in
       its row rather than the quiet half of a header, so a full-bleed button is the ask being
       obvious rather than the ask shouting. */
    .cred__cta {
      align-self: stretch;
    }

    .cred__cta .bo-btn {
      width: 100%;
      text-align: center;
    }
  }
</style>
